1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,871
The first thing Albert Wesker did with the second
tier was to put two people in a room

2
00:00:14,891 --> 00:00:16,789
and stand at the back of it.

3
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:23,376
Dr. William Birkin had been given the largest
laboratory complex in the western hemisphere

4
00:00:23,396 --> 00:00:29,237
and had spent six years filling it with the
scientific equivalent of a magpie's nest:

5
00:00:29,257 --> 00:00:35,374
four hundred programmes, none of them finished, all
of them brilliant in the first eleven

6
00:00:35,394 --> 00:00:35,787
pages.

7
00:00:36,188 --> 00:00:42,360
Dr. Evelyn Marsh had spent four months alone with a
freezer archive, and had found in

8
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:44,838
it a cell line that would not die.

9
00:00:45,237 --> 00:00:51,620
The line was labelled *ODD — DO NOT DISCARD*,
collected in 2004 by a technician who had

10
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:57,949
never lived, from a donor who had never existed.
Human dermal fibroblasts. They should

11
00:00:57,969 --> 00:01:04,278
have stopped dividing at forty passages, which is
what human cells do: they count, and

12
00:01:04,298 --> 00:01:06,928
when the count runs out they retire.

13
00:01:07,328 --> 00:01:12,478
These had reached three hundred and twenty-two
passages and had not aged a day.

14
00:01:12,998 --> 00:01:17,757
It isn't immortalisation. That's the first thing I
checked, that's what everyone checks.

15
00:01:17,777 --> 00:01:22,427
Immortalised lines are cancer — they've thrown away
the brakes and they drift, they go

16
00:01:22,447 --> 00:01:27,369
aneuploid, they turn into something that isn't a
person any more. These are *clean*. Normal

17
00:01:27,389 --> 00:01:31,877
karyotype at passage three hundred. They keep the
brakes. They just don't wear out.

18
00:01:32,277 --> 00:01:33,067
Telomerase?

19
00:01:33,466 --> 00:01:37,066
Off. Barely detectable. That's the part
that made me sit down.

20
00:01:37,465 --> 00:01:38,715
Then how are they maintaining —

21
00:01:39,115 --> 00:01:44,369
They aren't maintaining length. They're maintaining
*state*. The telomeres shorten exactly

22
00:01:44,389 --> 00:01:49,818
the way yours do, and the cell doesn't care, because
whatever is in these cells has uncoupled

23
00:01:49,838 --> 00:01:55,325
the damage signal from the shortening. They stop
counting. They don't stop dividing correctly.

24
00:01:55,725 --> 00:02:01,957
There was a silence of about four seconds, which in a
room containing William Birkin was

25
00:02:01,977 --> 00:02:03,236
almost unheard of.

26
00:02:03,756 --> 00:02:06,386
Oh, that's *filthy*. That's beautiful.
Albert, did you hear —

27
00:02:06,786 --> 00:02:12,242
Wesker had been standing at the back of the room with
his arms folded for fifteen minutes,

28
00:02:12,262 --> 00:02:17,414
and had not said a word, because he had understood
it in the first ninety seconds and

29
00:02:17,434 --> 00:02:21,855
had been waiting to see whether the two of
them would get there together.

30
00:02:22,376 --> 00:02:23,280
I heard.

31
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,109
He crossed to the screen.

32
00:02:25,629 --> 00:02:32,162
Dr. Marsh, you are describing a factor that
suppresses the senescence programme without

33
00:02:32,182 --> 00:02:38,715
disabling the tumour suppressors that make
senescence necessary. If that is true, it is

34
00:02:38,735 --> 00:02:45,116
the most valuable molecule on this planet, and if
it is *not* true, you have found an

35
00:02:45,136 --> 00:02:51,518
elaborate way to grow tumours slowly. So we will
find out which, and we will do it in

36
00:02:51,538 --> 00:02:55,359
the order that makes a product
rather than a paper.

37
00:02:55,759 --> 00:02:57,369
The order that makes a product.

38
00:02:57,770 --> 00:03:04,304
Fractionate the conditioned medium. Find the active
fraction before you find the mechanism.

39
00:03:04,324 --> 00:03:10,426
The mechanism is a decade of somebody's career; the
fraction is eleven weeks, and the

40
00:03:10,446 --> 00:03:13,380
fraction is what you dose an animal with.

41
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:14,629
That's backwards.

42
00:03:15,030 --> 00:03:21,829
That is *commerce*, Will. You may have the
mechanism afterwards, as a gift, with your

43
00:03:21,849 --> 00:03:25,439
name on it. I want the fraction
by September.

44
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:28,129
He had it by the second week of August.

45
00:03:28,530 --> 00:03:34,259
The narrator would like to move through eight months
quickly, because Albert Wesker did.

46
00:03:34,659 --> 00:03:40,346
The active fraction was a protein complex of four
subunits. Birkin's people rebuilt it

47
00:03:40,366 --> 00:03:45,986
as a single engineered molecule that could be made
in a vat; Voss's vector platform —

48
00:03:46,006 --> 00:03:51,692
the one Wesker had fixed in one evening in September
— delivered it; Pell's regulatory

49
00:03:51,712 --> 00:03:57,464
group took it into a first-in-human trial in a
hundred and forty volunteers, aged sixty

50
00:03:57,484 --> 00:04:03,503
to eighty-one, under a protocol so conservative that
the reviewers complained it was timid.

51
00:04:03,902 --> 00:04:09,952
They called it Aeterna, which Catherine hated and
which tested extraordinarily well.

52
00:04:10,353 --> 00:04:17,413
And in March of 2028 the first readout came back,
and a contract research organisation

53
00:04:17,433 --> 00:04:20,213
in Delaware quietly lost its mind.

54
00:04:20,612 --> 00:04:27,364
Thirty-one weeks of dosing. Two hundred and nine
measured markers of biological ageing:

55
00:04:27,384 --> 00:04:34,057
immune repertoire, inflammatory load, methylation
clocks, grip strength, VO2 max, skin

56
00:04:34,077 --> 00:04:35,302
recoil, the lot.

57
00:04:35,701 --> 00:04:38,567
The placebo arm had aged thirty-one weeks.

58
00:04:38,968 --> 00:04:45,009
The treatment arm had gone backwards. Not dramatically
— not a woman of seventy-two walking

59
00:04:45,029 --> 00:04:50,938
out with the face of a girl. On the clocks, an
average of nine years. In the immune data,

60
00:04:50,958 --> 00:04:56,866
more than that: the volunteers' T-cell repertoires
had begun refilling from a compartment

61
00:04:56,886 --> 00:04:59,931
that should have been closed for
four decades.

62
00:05:00,331 --> 00:05:06,322
A sixty-eight-year-old retired school administrator
named Doris Halloran, who had joined

63
00:05:06,342 --> 00:05:12,198
the trial because it paid four hundred dollars and
she liked the nurse, had the immune

64
00:05:12,218 --> 00:05:14,111
profile of a woman of forty.

65
00:05:14,511 --> 00:05:21,312
The data release was the first genuinely public thing
Umbrella ever did, and Wesker planned

66
00:05:21,332 --> 00:05:22,587
it for six weeks.

67
00:05:22,987 --> 00:05:29,106
He did not announce a miracle. He released the entire
dataset — every patient, every marker,

68
00:05:29,126 --> 00:05:34,778
the protocol, the failed assays, the two serious
adverse events, the statistical plan

69
00:05:34,798 --> 00:05:40,450
they had pre-registered twenty-seven months earlier
— and then he put Evelyn Marsh on

70
00:05:40,470 --> 00:05:46,255
a stage in front of nine hundred journalists and
four hundred scientists and let her be

71
00:05:46,275 --> 00:05:48,124
brilliant for forty minutes.

72
00:05:48,524 --> 00:05:50,033
Then he took the questions.

73
00:05:50,434 --> 00:05:56,197
There was a panel of hostile immunologists in the
third row. One of them, a man from a

74
00:05:56,217 --> 00:06:01,981
Boston institute who had spent a career on
senescence and had four competing molecules

75
00:06:02,001 --> 00:06:06,824
in trials, stood up with a question he had
sharpened on the flight down.

76
00:06:07,343 --> 00:06:12,163
Dr. Wesker, you've reported a nine-year reversal on
methylation clocks. Methylation clocks

77
00:06:12,183 --> 00:06:16,734
are correlational. They predict mortality in
populations; they don't measure anything

78
00:06:16,754 --> 00:06:21,736
in a person. You've moved a number on a graph. What
have you actually done to these patients?

79
00:06:22,134 --> 00:06:26,215
A murmur. It was a good question and
everybody in the room knew it.

80
00:06:26,735 --> 00:06:28,005
I agree with you.

81
00:06:28,404 --> 00:06:29,615
The murmur stopped.

82
00:06:30,134 --> 00:06:36,739
The clocks are the least interesting data in that
release, and if they were all we had

83
00:06:36,759 --> 00:06:43,596
I would not have taken this stage. Turn to the immune
panel, table eleven. Thymic output.

84
00:06:43,616 --> 00:06:50,144
You know as well as I do that the thymus involutes
in adolescence and is functionally

85
00:06:50,164 --> 00:06:57,000
gone by sixty, and that everything that makes an old
person's infection dangerous follows

86
00:06:57,020 --> 00:07:03,779
from that: no new T-cell receptors, so no new answers
to new pathogens, only the answers

87
00:07:03,799 --> 00:07:05,704
you had in your thirties.

88
00:07:05,985 --> 00:07:13,201
Table thirteen reports naive CD4 counts and receptor
excision circles at thirty-one weeks.

89
00:07:13,221 --> 00:07:20,277
Forty-one of our sixty-nine treated patients have
output that should not exist in a body

90
00:07:20,297 --> 00:07:27,112
of that age. That is not a correlation. That is a
tissue which had closed, reopening,

91
00:07:27,132 --> 00:07:34,670
in a living human being, and you may verify it in your
own laboratory because I have published

92
00:07:34,690 --> 00:07:35,475
the assay.

93
00:07:35,875 --> 00:07:37,145
And the mechanism?

94
00:07:37,545 --> 00:07:43,960
I do not know the mechanism, and I have declined to
guess in public, and I notice that

95
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:50,321
this is the first time in eight years anyone at
this podium has said that sentence to

96
00:07:50,341 --> 00:07:56,981
you. Dr. Marsh will publish the fractionation in the
autumn. She will be wrong about part

97
00:07:57,001 --> 00:08:02,295
of it and you will correct her, and that is
how it is supposed to work.

98
00:08:02,694 --> 00:08:08,339
The narrator wishes to record that the applause at
the end of that session was started,

99
00:08:08,359 --> 00:08:11,725
involuntarily, by the man from the
Boston institute.

100
00:08:12,125 --> 00:08:19,301
Umbrella Life Sciences had listed on the NASDAQ in
January at a valuation of two billion

101
00:08:19,321 --> 00:08:26,744
dollars, which Catherine Adeyemi had fought for and
which Wesker had considered insultingly

102
00:08:26,764 --> 00:08:27,235
small.

103
00:08:27,634 --> 00:08:33,445
Nine trading days after the Aeterna release, it
was worth sixty-one billion.

104
00:08:33,845 --> 00:08:40,144
Six competitors lost a combined forty per cent. Two
longevity startups in California folded

105
00:08:40,164 --> 00:08:46,394
inside a month, not because their science was bad but
because no fund on earth would write

106
00:08:46,414 --> 00:08:52,505
another cheque in that space when the answer was
already on a stage in Philadelphia with

107
00:08:52,525 --> 00:08:53,755
a published assay.

108
00:08:54,154 --> 00:09:00,164
A Swiss pharmaceutical house made an approach about
licensing. A sovereign wealth fund

109
00:09:00,184 --> 00:09:06,263
offered eleven billion for nineteen per cent and was
refused. A senator's office called

110
00:09:06,283 --> 00:09:07,034
Pell twice.

111
00:09:07,554 --> 00:09:11,956
You understand that you are now personally the
fourth-richest human being alive, on paper,

112
00:09:11,976 --> 00:09:13,971
and that by Friday you will be the first.

113
00:09:14,370 --> 00:09:15,461
On paper.

114
00:09:15,860 --> 00:09:20,123
Don't do that. Don't do the thing where the number is
beneath you. That number is a weapon

115
00:09:20,143 --> 00:09:21,646
and you should hold it properly.

116
00:09:22,046 --> 00:09:27,053
Wesker turned from the window and looked at her, and
the narrator notes that she was one

117
00:09:27,073 --> 00:09:30,995
of perhaps three people in the world who
would have said that to him.

118
00:09:31,515 --> 00:09:33,905
Then tell me what it buys.

119
00:09:34,306 --> 00:09:38,665
It buys the end of anyone's ability to say no to
you commercially. Every regulator on

120
00:09:38,685 --> 00:09:43,044
earth now has a queue of citizens outside demanding
the thing you have. Every finance

121
00:09:43,064 --> 00:09:47,423
ministry has costed what a nine-year immune
reversal does to their pension liability,

122
00:09:47,443 --> 00:09:51,957
and half of them have realised it makes them
*solvent*. You are no longer a company that

123
00:09:51,977 --> 00:09:56,388
sells a test. You are a strategic asset of about
nine countries, and none of them know

124
00:09:56,408 --> 00:10:00,716
what to do about it, and while they work it out you
can do almost anything you like.

125
00:10:01,115 --> 00:10:02,566
How long is "while"?

126
00:10:02,966 --> 00:10:04,495
Two years. Maybe three.

127
00:10:04,895 --> 00:10:06,525
He was quiet for a moment.

128
00:10:07,046 --> 00:10:10,256
Then I will need territory by 2030.

129
00:10:10,655 --> 00:10:11,975
Territory.

130
00:10:12,375 --> 00:10:15,625
Goodnight, Ms. Adeyemi.

131
00:10:16,025 --> 00:10:21,656
The narrator will give you the world in five
pictures, because this is the year the world

132
00:10:21,676 --> 00:10:22,735
starts to matter.

133
00:10:23,135 --> 00:10:29,085
In Singapore, a private clinic took deposits for a
therapy that was two years from approval,

134
00:10:29,105 --> 00:10:32,005
and took eighteen thousand of
them in a week.

135
00:10:32,405 --> 00:10:38,851
In Ohio, a pharmacist put a printed notice on an
empty shelf explaining, for the ninth

136
00:10:38,871 --> 00:10:45,392
time that day, that Aeterna was not a pill, was not
for sale, and would not be for sale

137
00:10:45,412 --> 00:10:51,105
until at least 2030, and that no, she did not
know anybody who could get it.

138
00:10:51,505 --> 00:10:56,919
In Rome, a cardinal preached a careful sermon about
the difference between healing and

139
00:10:56,939 --> 00:11:02,480
refusing to die, and was quoted approvingly by people
who had not listened to the second

140
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:02,796
half.

141
00:11:03,196 --> 00:11:08,957
In a committee room in Washington, a health economist
testified that if the trial result

142
00:11:08,977 --> 00:11:14,804
held at scale, the United States' long-term care
liability fell by something between four

143
00:11:14,824 --> 00:11:20,586
and twenty-four trillion dollars, and watched every
member of the committee stop looking

144
00:11:20,606 --> 00:11:22,886
at their phones at the same moment.

145
00:11:23,285 --> 00:11:29,309
And in a dormitory in Manila, a nineteen-year-old
biology student changed the wallpaper

146
00:11:29,329 --> 00:11:35,214
on her phone to a photograph of Evelyn Marsh on a
stage in Philadelphia, and told her

147
00:11:35,234 --> 00:11:38,965
mother she was going to work for
those people one day.

148
00:11:39,365 --> 00:11:45,390
Nobody was frightened. That is the thing to hold on
to. In the spring of 2028, not one

149
00:11:45,410 --> 00:11:51,787
human being on this planet was frightened of Umbrella,
and the reason is simple: everything

150
00:11:51,807 --> 00:11:53,895
it had done so far was *good*.

151
00:11:55,046 --> 00:12:03,528
Corporate growth. Valuation event. Sixty-one point
four billion dollars. Product of global

152
00:12:03,548 --> 00:12:11,841
consequence: registered. One million four hundred and
ten thousand Points. Level twenty.

153
00:12:11,861 --> 00:12:14,581
Thirty stat points available.

154
00:12:15,169 --> 00:12:18,090
One million four hundred and ten thousand.

155
00:12:18,490 --> 00:12:24,321
He had built the entire first tier on nine thousand. He
had bought a campus for thirty-four.

156
00:12:24,341 --> 00:12:29,919
He looked at a number with six zeros in it, awarded
for a single quarter, and understood

157
00:12:29,939 --> 00:12:35,389
that the System had just told him something about
its own priorities that it had never

158
00:12:35,409 --> 00:12:36,280
said out loud.

159
00:12:36,799 --> 00:12:39,250
You do not pay for products.

160
00:12:39,649 --> 00:12:40,399
No.

161
00:12:40,799 --> 00:12:47,291
You pay for *consequence*. The assay saves more lives
than Aeterna will and it was worth

162
00:12:47,311 --> 00:12:53,950
fourteen thousand. This is worth a hundred times that
because it moved a pension liability

163
00:12:53,970 --> 00:12:55,429
in a committee room.

164
00:12:55,830 --> 00:12:57,508
You are learning to read me.

165
00:12:57,908 --> 00:13:05,158
I have been reading you since September. Twenty-two
into Intelligence. Eight into Will.

166
00:13:06,187 --> 00:13:13,608
Status update. Intelligence, thirty-four to
forty-two. Will, thirty-two to thirty-eight.

167
00:13:13,628 --> 00:13:19,273
At Intelligence forty-two you will begin
to find other people slow.

168
00:13:19,863 --> 00:13:25,155
The note was not a warning. It was an
advertisement, and he knew it, and he bought it

169
00:13:25,175 --> 00:13:25,593
anyway.

170
00:13:25,993 --> 00:13:28,963
Something behind his eyes turned
over, and spoke.

171
00:13:29,482 --> 00:13:31,557
You have made them love you.

172
00:13:31,956 --> 00:13:37,726
I have made them *want* something. Love is what they
will call it later, when they cannot

173
00:13:37,746 --> 00:13:38,247
have it.

174
00:13:38,647 --> 00:13:40,796
And when they cannot have it?

175
00:13:41,197 --> 00:13:46,992
Wesker looked down at the river, and at five
buildings that had appeared over a weekend,

176
00:13:47,012 --> 00:13:50,626
and at a city that had never once
asked him a question.

177
00:13:51,147 --> 00:13:53,037
Then I will own the queue.
